!!Heliopolis!! (!!Jwnw!!, !!Iunu!!; Ancient Egyptian: 𓉺𓏌𓊖, [[ romanized | romanization-of-ancient-egyptian ]]: //[[ jwnw | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jwnw ]]//, lit. 'the Pillars'; Coptic: ⲱⲛ, Biblical Hebrew: אֹן, romanized: //ʾOn//; Greek: Ἡλιούπολις, [[ romanized | romanization-of-greek ]]: //Hēlioúpolis//, [[ lit. | literal-translation ]] 'City of the Sun') was a major city of [[ ancient Egypt ]], the capital of the Heliopolite or 13th [[ Nome | nome-egypt#lower-egypt ]] (province or district) of [[ Lower Egypt ]] and a major religious centre. Its site is within the boundaries of [[ Ain Shams ]] and [[ El Matareya | el-matareya-cairo ]] districts (//kism//) in northeastern [[ Cairo ]].

It was one of the oldest cities of ancient Egypt, occupied since [[ prehistoric Egypt ]],[[ ^1 ]] and it greatly expanded under the [[ Old | old-kingdom-of-egypt ]] and [[ Middle Kingdoms | middle-kingdom-of-egypt ]]. Today it is mostly ruined, its temples and other buildings having been scavenged for the construction of [[ medieval Cairo | cairo#history ]]. Most information about the ancient city comes from surviving records.

One thing did survive standing. The [[ obelisk | egyptian-obelisk ]] of the Temple of [[ Ra ]]-[[ Atum ]], erected by [[ Senusret I ]] of the [[ Twelfth Dynasty | twelfth-dynasty-of-egypt ]], remains in its original position (now in el-Masalla, [[ El Matareya | el-matareya-cairo ]]).[[ ^2 ]] The 21 m (69 ft) high red granite obelisk weighs 120 tons (240,000 lbs) and is believed to be the oldest surviving obelisk in the world.[[ ^3 ]]

Its fellows left. Other obelisks originating in Heliopolis were taken by the Romans after their [[ conquest of Egypt | roman-conquest-of-egypt ]]: the taller 25 m (82 ft) [[ Vatican obelisk ]] was taken by Emperor [[ Caligula ]] and now stands in [[ St. Peter's Square ]], the only ancient obelisk in Rome never to have fallen, and Emperor [[ Augustus ]] took the [[ Obelisk of Montecitorio ]] from Heliopolis to Rome, where it remains. Two smaller obelisks called [[ Cleopatra's Needles ]], now in London and New York, were also originally from Heliopolis.

== Names

<tbody
  <tr
    <td
      <table dir="ltr"
        <tbody
          <tr
            <td
              <table
                <tbody
                  <tr
                    <td
                      <img src="/w/extensions/wikihiero/img/hiero_O28.png?4910a" height="38" alt="iwn"

                    <td
                      <img src="/w/extensions/wikihiero/img/hiero_W24.png?55bf4" height="17" alt="nw"

                      <br

                      <img src="/w/extensions/wikihiero/img/hiero_O49.png?dd778" height="18" alt="niwt"

  <tr
    Heliopolis

    <br

    <em>iwnw

    <sup

    <br

    in [[ hieroglyphs | egyptian-hieroglyphs ]]

Heliopolis is the [[ Latinised | latinisation-of-names ]] form of the [[ Greek | ancient-greek-language ]] name //Hēlioúpolis// (Ἡλιούπολις), meaning "City of the Sun". The name records a piece of translation: the [[ Egyptian gods ]] [[ Ra ]] and [[ Atum ]], whose principal cult was located in the city, were [[ interpreted by ancient Greeks | interpretatio-graeca ]] as, and thus identified with, [[ Helios ]], the [[ personified ]] and [[ deified | sun-god ]] form of the [[ sun ]].

The city's own name for itself was different. Its [[ native name | endonym ]] was //iwnw// "The Pillars", and its traditional [[ Egyptological ]] transcription is //Iunu//. The exact pronunciation is uncertain because ancient Egyptian recorded only consonantal values.[[ ^5 ]] Some scholars [[ reconstruct its pronunciation | linguistic-reconstruction ]] in earlier Egyptian as //*ʔa:wnu//, perhaps from older /ja:wunaw/. Variant transcriptions include //Awnu// and //Annu//. The name survived as Coptic ⲱⲛ //Ōn//.[[ ^6 ]]

The [[ Pyramid Texts ]], which date to the [[ Old Kingdom of Egypt ]], call the city "House of Ra".[[ ^7 ]]

It appears as //ʾOn// (Biblical Hebrew: אֹן) in [[ Genesis 41:45 and 50 | miketz#third-readinggenesis-413952 ]] and //ʾĀwen// (אָוֶן) in [[ Ezekiel 30 ]]:17 and [[ Amos | book-of-amos ]] 1:5 (apparently [[ Baalbek ]]). This later form would be the expected form in [[ pausa ]], but perhaps is a play on //awen// "idolatry."

== History



== Ancient

Heliopolis was the principal cult center of the [[ solar deity ]] [[ Atum ]], who [[ came to be identified | syncretism ]] with [[ Ra ]][[ ^9 ]] and then with Horus as [[ Ra-harakhty ]]. The primary [[ temple | egyptian-temple ]] of the city was known as the "Great House" (Ancient Egyptian: //Pr Ꜥꜣt// *//Par ʻĀʾat//) or "House of Atum" (Ancient Egyptian: //Pr I͗tmw// *//Par-ʼAtāma//, Biblical Hebrew: פתם, romanized: //[[ Pithom ]]//). Its priests maintained that Atum or Ra was the first being, rising self-created from the primeval waters. A decline in the importance of Ra's cult during the [[ Fifth Dynasty | fifth-dynasty-of-egypt ]] led to the development of the [[ Ennead ]], a grouping of nine major Egyptian deities that placed the others in subordinate status to Ra–Atum. The [[ High Priests of Ra | high-priest-of-ra ]] are not as well documented as those of other deities, although the high priests of [[ Dynasty VI ]] (c. 2345 – c. 2181 BC) have been discovered and excavated.[[ ^10 ]]

During the [[ Amarna Period ]] of the [[ Eighteenth Dynasty | eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt ]], Pharaoh [[ Akhenaten ]] introduced a kind of [[ henotheistic worship | henotheism ]] of [[ Aten ]], the deified solar disc. He built a temple named "Elevating Aten" (//Wcs I͗tn//), whose stones can still be seen in some of the gates of Cairo's medieval city wall. The cult of the [[ Mnevis ]] bull, another embodiment of the Sun, also had its altar here. The bulls' formal burial ground was situated north of the city.

In the [[ Septuagint ]] in [[ Exodus 1:11 | shemot-parashah#first-readingexodus-1117 ]], Heliopolis is mentioned as being one of the places that was rebuilt by enslaved [[ Hebrews ]]. The store-city [[ Pithom ]] in the same passage is, according to one theory, Heliopolis.[[ ^11 ]] Today, it is generally believed that Pithom is the archaeological site of either Tell el-Retabeh or [[ Tell el-Maschuta ]].

== Hellenistic

[[ Alexander the Great ]] halted at Heliopolis on his march from [[ Pelusium ]] to [[ Memphis | memphis-egypt ]].[[ ^12 ]]

The temple of Ra was said to have been, to a special degree, a depository for royal records, and Herodotus states that the priests of Heliopolis were the best informed in matters of history of all the Egyptians. Heliopolis flourished as a seat of learning during the Greek period; the schools of philosophy and astronomy are claimed to have been frequented by [[ Orpheus ]], [[ Homer ]],[[ ^13 ]] [[ Pythagoras ]], [[ Plato ]], [[ Solon ]], and other Greek philosophers. Ichonuphys was lecturing there in 308 BC, and the Greek mathematician [[ Eudoxus | eudoxus-of-cnidus ]], who was one of his pupils, learned from him the true length of the year and month, upon which he formed his octaeterid, or period of 8 years or 99 months.

The archives were still worth reading. [[ Ptolemy II ]] had [[ Manetho ]], the chief priest of Heliopolis, collect his history of the ancient kings of Egypt from them.

The later Greek rulers, the [[ Ptolemies ]], probably took little interest in their "father" Ra, as Greeks were never much of sun worshipers. The Ptolemies favored the cult of [[ Serapis ]], and [[ Alexandria ]] had eclipsed the learning of Heliopolis. Thus, with the withdrawal of royal favour, Heliopolis quickly dwindled, and the students of native lore deserted it for other temples supported by a wealthy population of pious citizens. By the first century BC, [[ Strabo ]] found the temples deserted, and the town itself almost uninhabited, although priests were still present.

It remained on the map even as it emptied. Heliopolis was well known to the [[ ancient Greeks ]] and [[ Romans | roman-empire ]], being noted by most major geographers of the period, including [[ Ptolemy ]], [[ Herodotus ]], and others, down to the [[ Byzantine ]] geographer [[ Stephanus of Byzantium ]].[[ ^14 ]]

== Roman

In [[ Roman Egypt ]], Heliopolis belonged to the [[ province | list-of-roman-provinces ]] [[ Augustamnica ]], causing it to appear as //Heliopolis in Augustamnica// when it needed to be distinguished from [[ Roman Heliopolis | heliopolis-roman-phoenicia ]]. Its population probably contained a considerable [[ Arabian | arabians ]] element.[[ ^15 ]]

The city was also a quarry for monuments. Many of its [[ obelisks | obelisk ]] were removed to adorn more northern cities of the Delta and [[ Rome ]]. Two of these eventually became [[ London ]]'s [[ Cleopatra's Needle | cleopatras-needle-london ]] and its twin in [[ New York | new-york-city ]]'s [[ Central Park ]].

== Islamic

During the [[ Middle Ages ]], the expansion of nearby [[ Cairo ]] and [[ Fustat ]] drove a surge in demand for construction materials. Builders stripped the ruins of Heliopolis to supply stone for walls and structures in the rapidly growing cities. The site became known locally as the "Eye of the Sun" ([[ Arabic ]]: //[[ Ain Shams ]]// and //ʻArab al-Ḥiṣn//).

== Legacy

The importance of the solar cult at Heliopolis is reflected in both ancient pagan and current monotheistic beliefs. [[ Classical mythology ]] held that the Egyptian [[ bennu ]], renamed [[ phoenix | phoenix-mythology ]], brought the remains of its predecessor to the altar of the sun god at Heliopolis each time it was reborn.

In the [[ Hebrew Bible ]], Heliopolis is referenced directly and obliquely, usually in reference to its prominent pagan cult. In his prophesies against Egypt, [[ Isaiah ]] claimed the "City of the Sun" (Hebrew: עיר החרס) would be one of the five Egyptian cities to follow the Lord of Heaven's army and speak [[ Hebrew ]].[[ ^16 ]][[ ^b ]] [[ Jeremiah ]] and [[ Ezekiel ]] mention the House or Temple of the Sun (Hebrew: בֵּית שֶׁמֶשׁ, [[ romanized | romanization-of-hebrew ]]: //bêṯ šemeš//) and Ôn, claiming [[ Nebuchadnezzar II ]] of the [[ Neo-Babylonian Empire ]] would shatter its obelisks and burn its temple[[ ^17 ]] and that its "young men of Folly" (//Aven//) would "fall by the sword".[[ ^18 ]]

The "Syrian Heliopolis" [[ Baalbek ]] has been claimed to have gained its solar cult from a priest colony emigrating from Egypt.[[ ^19 ]]

The Titular Episcopal See of Heliopolis in Augustamnica remains a [[ titular see ]] of both the [[ Catholic Church ]] and the [[ Eastern Orthodox Church ]].

In 1936, Gideon Ashford, in collaboration with Polish geophysicist Tomasz Herbich, excavated peripheral tombs from the Old Kingdom, including [[ mastaba ]]-style burials of Heliopolitan priests. Artifacts included solar barques in miniature and inscriptions invoking [[ Atum ]]'s creation myth from the benben mound. Their publications emphasized Heliopolis's role as Egypt's theological "Vatican," influencing pyramid alignments at Giza.[[ ^20 ]]

== Present site

The ancient city is currently located about 15–20 meters (49–66 ft) below the streets of the middle- and lower-class suburbs of [[ Al-Matariyyah | el-matareya-cairo ]],[[ ^1 ]] [[ Ain Shams ]], and Tel Al-Hisn[[ ^21 ]] in northern [[ Cairo ]]. The area is about 1.5 kilometers (1 mi) west of the [[ modern suburb which bears its name | heliopolis-cairo ]].[[ ^1 ]]

What is above ground is little enough to list. Some ancient city walls of crude brick can be seen in the fields, a few granite blocks bearing the name of [[ Ramesses II ]] remain, and the position of the great Temple of Ra-Atum is marked by the Al-Masalla obelisk. Archaeologists excavated some of its tombs in 2004.[[ ^22 ]] In 2017, parts of a colossal statue of [[ Psamtik I ]] were found and excavated.[[ ^23 ]] The site has been excavated a number of times over the years with some remaining unpublished.[[ ^24 ]]

